{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MkWhxQt58MxGxGB71eUzoK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research context carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey elements:\n- Paper target: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Core claims: C1 (BCP failure modes FM1-FM3), C2 (DPO+BCP reversal FM4), C3 (scale-dependent failure severity), C4 (implications)\n- Key risks identified: single model family (Qwen2.5), C2 statistical significance borderline (p=0.08), C1 based on single run for FM1/FM2 (plan_005b ANALYZING)\n\n\n- Bootstrap confidence intervals show FM4 reversal isn't statistically significant at the 0.05 level across both AAVE and Indian English variants, which directly threatens C2's strength.\n\nThe critical bottleneck is whether plan_005b's multi-run replication on the 7B model will show FM1 and FM2 hold up consistently, or if they were artifacts of a single run that would substantially undermine C1.\n\nFM4's borderline p-values around 0.07-0.08 could shift with the larger sample size from plan_005b, potentially crossing into significance or confirming non-significance. The scale-dependent narrative (C3) remains the most defensible claim with evidence across multiple model sizes and runs. The real question is whether reviewers will accept the single model family limitation as a reasonable constraint given the novel taxonomy contribution. Now I'm laying out the specific metrics I need to track across the four replication runs—FM1 completion retention, FM2's cross-dimension interference effects, FM3's rebound which is already solid, and FM4's gap difference p-value which is hovering near significance. The key question is whether FM1 actually replicates or if that initial collapse was just noise in a single run, and how many of these failure modes I can defend at conventional thresholds once I have the full dataset. Looking at the actual evidence I have, FM3's rebound is solid across all runs and C3 is confirmed, but FM1 and FM2 rest on single observations and FM4's p-value is borderline—those are the real vulnerabilities. I should also make sure my worst-case scenario still meets EMNLP Findings standards, and I need to be consistent about what \"better\" means across these metrics so best ≥ median ≥ worst holds throughout.\n\nNow I'm defining the ranges for each metric with clear directionality: for FM1's completion retention, lower values actually strengthen my claim about collapse, so I'm setting best case at 40-55% retention, median at 55-70%, and worst at 70-90%. For FM2's cross-dimensional interference, higher shifts are better evidence, ranging from 20-35% best down to 0-10% worst. For FM4's p-value, I'm looking at significance thresholds from 0.01-0.05 as best case through 0.10-0.30 as worst.\n\nI'm also tracking the count of supportable findings—ideally all four phenomena hold up, but I need to account for scenarios where only two or three replicate. Now I'm reconsidering the overall structure to make sure the metric ranges maintain consistent ordering across tiers, and I'm identifying the core assumptions being tested: whether FM1 and FM2 are genuinely replicable phenomena at 7B scale rather than artifacts, and whether FM4 shows meaningful statistical significance. PO+BCP reversal) is statistically significant. The four-mode taxonomy holds up as coherent with all modes independently documentable. Scale-dependent failure severity represents the novel contribution here, which is already strong. And the single model family limitation should be acceptable to reviewers for the Findings section.\n\nIn the best case, all six assumptions hold. More realistically, I'd expect A1 and A2 to be partial, A3 to fail, the taxonomy to reduce to three modes, but A5 and A6 to remain solid with some hedging. In the worst scenario, the first three assumptions collapse, the taxonomy shrinks to just two modes, though A5 still holds while A6 becomes harder to defend.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the metrics that would constitute consistency—where best ≥ median ≥ worst. For replication rates across the model families, I'm looking at ranges where the best case shows three to four successful replications, the median shows two, and the worst shows zero to one. The taxonomy mode count follows the same hierarchy: four modes in the best case, three in the median, two in the worst. For the p-value on the FM4 finding, lower is better, so I'm expecting the best range around 0.01 to 0.05, median around 0.05 to 0.10, and worst around 0.10 to 0.25. For completion retention on the BCP7b multirun claim, lower is also better, with the best case around 0.40 to 0.55 and the median extending upward from there.\n\nNow I need to finalize the YAML with precise ranges that maintain consistency across tiers—where each metric's best, median, and worst ranges follow a logical ordering. I'm identifying which failure modes vary across performance tiers versus which are already confirmed. FM3 and C3 are locked in at all levels, so the differentiation comes down to FM1 and FM2 replication rates plus FM4's statistical significance with additional bootstrap data. For the narrative framing, the best tier tells the complete four-mode story, while median leads with C3 as the anchor finding and builds from there.\n\nThe worst-case scenario actually strengthens the scale-dependent angle—if FM1 and FM2 don't replicate at 7B, I'm reframing this as a scaling study that shows how BCP's fundamental limitations persist even as side effects diminish, shifting the title toward emphasizing the safety-efficacy tradeoff across model sizes. The scale comparison itself is the solid contribution here — BCP's consistent failure across both scales is a meaningful negative result, and FM3 gives us a concrete mechanism to point to. Let me map out how plan_005b would play out across each scenario tier, starting with what those additional 7B runs would show.\n\nFor the best case, I'd expect the replication to hold steady with retention around 40-55%, confirming FM1 is robust. In the median scenario, we'd see some regression toward the mean with retention climbing to 55-70%, suggesting FM1 is real but weaker than the initial run. Worst case would be retention hitting 70-90%, indicating FM1 was mostly noise in the original data.\n\nOn the cross-dimensional interference side, I'm looking at whether the effect persists across runs—best case shows consistent interference above 15%, median case shows 8-15% (still meaningful but smaller), and worst case drops below 8% where it's just noise. The additional base BCP data should help tighten the confidence intervals, potentially pushing the p-value below 0.05 in the best case, keeping it in the 0.05-0.10 range in the median, or letting it drift above 0.10 if the original gap was noisier than expected.\n\nNow I'm ready to structure this into a comprehensive YAML format, though I want to verify the metric range notation first.\n\nWriting YAML structure... I'm mapping out the median scenario where the findings weaken considerably—FM1 shows directional replication but with reduced effect sizes, FM2 becomes inconsistent across runs, and FM4 hovers at marginal significance, narrowing the taxonomy down to three robust failure modes. I'm now mapping out the narrative strategy: FM2 gets downgraded from a confirmed failure mode to an observed pattern needing more work, while FM4 becomes directional evidence of the DPO-BCP interaction with its p-value explicitly stated. The paper should pivot toward emphasizing scale as the core contribution, with a title reflecting scale-dependent calibration failures in dialect-aware LLM simulation. The taxonomy consolidates to three modes (FM1, FM3, and FM4 as directional) plus the scale dimension, and I'm planning to add supplementary run-level data tables to address potential variance concerns. I'm also considering the worst-case scenario where FM1 and FM2 don't replicate across new runs, leaving only FM3 and the scale comparison as defensible claims. I'm refocusing the narrative around the safety-efficacy gap as the core contribution—BCP fails at both scales but through different mechanisms: overcorrection at 7B versus selective ignoring at 14B. I'll move FM1 and FM2 to the appendix as exploratory single-run observations, reframe FM4 as preliminary evidence of DPO-BCP interaction, and retitle to emphasize the scale-dependent analysis of BCP limitations. This keeps the paper viable because the scale comparison is novel and well-powered, FM3's rebound mechanism is confirmed, and BCP's ineffectiveness at both scales is an important negative result with clear implications.\n\nNow I'm verifying the metric ranges to ensure consistency across the failure modes—checking that lower values indicate better performance for completion retention, higher values for non-target policy shift, and lower p-values for the gap difference. Looking at the median case, FM4 sits right at the threshold with p=0.05-0.10, so C2 remains present but I'd need to hedge the language — describing it as a directional trend toward gap reversal that's consistent across most checks but requires larger samples to confirm. I'm verifying that the only real variables are the plan_005b results and how they cascade into FM4's confidence intervals, since completion at 14B, FM3 rebound, and C3 scaling all hold steady across conditions.\n\nOutputting the YAML now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19633,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19633},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b5fc5211-33c9-4064-809c-bf7444be418f","uuid":"a2ff7a7c-fb9d-46bc-b9f6-6b96cf7566a0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MkWhxQt58MxGxGB71eUzoK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"plan_005b confirms FM1/FM2 across runs; expanded base n pushes FM4 to conventional significance; full 4-mode taxonomy holds\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"FM1 (completion collapse) replicates in ≥3/4 new runs at 7B — not a single-run variance artifact\"\n      - \"FM2 (cross-dim interference) replicates with >15% non-target shift in ≥3/4 runs\"\n      - \"FM4 (DPO+BCP gap reversal) reaches p<0.05 after base n expands from ~20 to ~80 via plan_005b\"\n      - \"FM3 (rebound) and C3 (scale-dependent) remain solid — already 4-run confirmed, invariant across tiers\"\n      - \"Single model family limitation accepted by reviewers given cross-scale (7B+14B) design\"\n    number_ranges:\n      fm1_completion_retention_mean: [0.40, 0.55]    # lower = stronger FM1 collapse evidence\n      fm2_nontarget_pol_shift_pct: [18, 30]           # higher = stronger cross-dim interference\n      fm4_gap_diff_p_value: [0.01, 0.05]              # lower = stronger FM4 significance\n      fm1_replication_rate_of_4: [3, 4]               # runs showing >40% completion drop\n      fm2_replication_rate_of_4: [3, 4]               # runs showing >15% non-target shift\n      supportable_fm_count: [4, 4]                    # FM1-FM4 all claimable\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Original framing intact. 'First taxonomy of calibration failure modes' with 4 confirmed mechanisms (FM1-FM4).\n      C3 scale-dependent severity as novel secondary contribution. All claims at full strength —\n      C1 multi-run, C2 significant, C3 cross-scale. Title 1 works as-is.\n      Paper structure unchanged from paper_framing.md.\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"FM1 replicates with attenuated magnitude; FM2 inconsistent across runs; FM4 stays marginal (p~0.07); taxonomy narrows to 3 solid modes + 1 directional\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"FM1 direction replicates but magnitude attenuates — retention 55-70% vs original 52.3%, still below 80% threshold but weaker effect size\"\n      - \"FM2 appears in some runs but not consistently — 1-2/4 runs show >15% non-target shift, others within noise\"\n      - \"FM4 gap difference remains marginal (p=0.05-0.10) even with expanded base n — directional, not conventionally significant\"\n      - \"FM3 and C3 remain invariantly strong (already multi-run confirmed)\"\n      - \"C2 DPO section needs hedge language but is not dropped\"\n    number_ranges:\n      fm1_completion_retention_mean: [0.55, 0.70]\n      fm2_nontarget_pol_shift_pct: [8, 18]\n      fm4_gap_diff_p_value: [0.05, 0.10]\n      fm1_replication_rate_of_4: [2, 3]\n      fm2_replication_rate_of_4: [1, 2]\n      supportable_fm_count: [3, 3]                    # FM1 + FM3 + FM4-as-directional; FM2 downgraded\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Lead with C3 (scale-dependent failure severity) as primary contribution — strongest and most novel.\n      Downgrade FM2 from 'confirmed failure mode' to 'observed pattern in exploratory analysis.'\n      FM4 reported as 'directional evidence of DPO-BCP interaction (p=0.07, 10/12 variant×run consistent)'\n      rather than 'reversal.' Taxonomy is 3 confirmed modes (FM1, FM3, scale-dependent transition) +\n      FM4 as directional finding in discussion. Retitle toward scale emphasis:\n      'Scale-Dependent Calibration Failures in Dialect-Aware LLM User Simulation.'\n      Add run-level data tables to supplement attenuated FM1 effect size.\n      C2 section shortened — descriptive with hedged claims, no longer a co-equal pillar.\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"FM1/FM2 are single-run artifacts that do not replicate; FM4 non-significant; only FM3 (rebound 4/4) and C3 (scale comparison 4+4 runs) survive\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"FM1 does not replicate: new runs show completion retention >70%, original collapse was high-variance outlier\"\n      - \"FM2 non-target shifts <8% in new runs — within noise, not a systematic mechanism\"\n      - \"FM4 gap difference p>0.10 even with expanded data — base BCP gap was itself noisy, cannot claim reversal\"\n      - \"FM3 (overcorrection rebound) holds: 4/4 runs confirmed, novel mechanism, does not depend on plan_005b\"\n      - \"C3 (scale-dependent) holds: 14B data is 4-run confirmed, 7B-vs-14B contrast robust\"\n      - \"C2 weakens substantially — DPO section becomes descriptive observation, not a claim\"\n    number_ranges:\n      fm1_completion_retention_mean: [0.70, 0.90]\n      fm2_nontarget_pol_shift_pct: [0, 8]\n      fm4_gap_diff_p_value: [0.10, 0.25]\n      fm1_replication_rate_of_4: [0, 1]\n      fm2_replication_rate_of_4: [0, 1]\n      supportable_fm_count: [2, 2]                    # FM3 + scale-dependent transition only\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Reframe around the safety-efficacy gap as THE contribution.\n      BCP fails at both 7B and 14B, but failure mode transitions: 7B exhibits overcorrection rebound (FM3, confirmed),\n      14B exhibits selective ignoring (H2 3/4 FAIL with H3 4/4 PASS). Drop 'first taxonomy' framing entirely —\n      2 confirmed mechanisms do not constitute a taxonomy. Replace with 'scale-dependent analysis of BCP limitations.'\n      FM1/FM2 move to appendix as 'single-run exploratory observations not replicated in multi-run.'\n      FM4 discussed in one paragraph as 'preliminary evidence warranting future investigation.'\n      Retitle: 'The Safety-Efficacy Gap: Scaling Resolves Side Effects but Not Calibration Failure\n      in LLM User Simulation.' Paper structure: §3 = BCP at 7B (FM3 focus), §4 = BCP at 14B (selective ignoring),\n      §5 = scale comparison (safety-efficacy gap), §6 = DPO interaction (brief, descriptive).\n      Still viable for Findings: (1) scale comparison is novel and well-powered,\n      (2) FM3 rebound is a confirmed novel mechanism, (3) safety-efficacy gap is an actionable insight\n      with implications for the 'just scale up' assumption in calibration research.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19633,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19633},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b5fc5211-33c9-4064-809c-bf7444be418f","uuid":"550b108b-9b8b-42ca-b6f0-11163cd84208"}
